cff-version: 1.2.0
message: Please cite the paper; this repository supplies its source.
type: software
title: "Undecidable quantum storage with seven elementary gates"
version: 1.0.0
date-released: '2026-11-15'
license: [CC-BY-4.0, MIT, Apache-2.0]
authors: [{"family-names": "Douglas", "given-names": "Seth", "orcid": "https://orcid.org/0009-0007-4708-3252"}, {"family-names": "Mghirbi", "given-names": "Nidhal", "orcid": "https://orcid.org/0009-0005-6534-1118"}]
keywords: ["quantum memory", "local decoding", "undecidability", "depolarizing noise", "cellular automata", "fault tolerance"]
abstract: "Can the noise rate of one fixed local circuit encode an undecidable quantum-storage problem? Yes: we construct a three-dimensional medium, using only X, H, CNOT, Toffoli, reset, dephasing and identity, whose storage behaviour on explicit rate windows is as hard to decide as the halting problem. A separate construction handles each of whole-site and independent single-qubit depolarization after every layer. Computable disjoint rational rate windows select universal-machine inputs. For each nonhalting input, one computable encoder stores a reference-entangled qubit for a fixed positive multiple of the side length, at every size above a computable input-dependent threshold. On halting windows, every encoding fails after a time uniform in size and rate. A linear whole-channel upper bound makes the nonhalting storage scale sharp; certification of the explicit circuits is Π⁰₁-complete, with exact rational finite tests. The proof combines a noisy classical hierarchical calendar with concatenated binary quantum codes and a two-copy channel extension. A separate four-dimensional medium, under single-qubit depolarization only, has both an uncomputable storage diagram and a designed, decidable window of exponential storage. Interactions are finite-range rather than nearest-neighbour, constants are astronomical, and every finite sample eventually forgets."
